// std.mem.mem_alloc
//
// LAW 1: there is NO ambient allocator. Anything that needs memory
// takes an Alloc, so "does this allocate?" is answered from the
// signature alone: no Alloc parameter, no allocation.
//
// Alloc is an INTERFACE — an ordinary struct whose fields happen to be
// functions, nothing marked special. An Alloc VALUE is the fat value
// every trait value is: receiver pointer plus one function pointer per
// method, stack-built, copied by value, never boxed or allocated.

Ptr = std.mem.mem_ptr

// One variant, so the bar LEADS: without it `AllocError = OutOfMemory`
// and `Alias = Shape` are the same three tokens. Exactly one way to
// run out of memory — law 4 says a reason is never invented.
AllocError* = | OutOfMemory

// The allocator interface. Implementations: an arena (env.mem.alloc(),
// the one this library ships), a fixed buffer, C's malloc.
//
// EVERY METHOD HERE TAKES `self: @Self`, NEVER `self :: @Self` — the
// load-bearing line in this module: an Alloc is a HANDLE, two words,
// copies freely, NOT Drop (the Arena behind it owns the memory and is
// what Drop applies to). Allocating mutates the arena, not the
// handle's own bytes, so a bitwise copy still sees the change —
// DESIGN.md's test for `:`.
//
// What it buys: Vec stores `alloc: Alloc` as an IMMUTABLE field and
// Vec.grow calls realloc through it. The other way, that call is
// illegal and every collection needs a mutable allocator field.
Alloc* = {
    // required (`= sig`, no body): an impl must provide these two;
    // everything else in std is built from them.
    //
    // raw is bytes only — size and alignment in, address out. Naming
    // what the bytes are is `Ptr.to`, one level down
    raw* = (self: @Self, size: usize, align: usize) Res<Ptr<u8>, AllocError>

    // `count` is the NEW element count. A null p is a fresh
    // allocation, letting an empty Vec grow through the same call as
    // a full one
    realloc* = <T>(self: @Self, p: Ptr<T>, count: usize) Res<Ptr<T>, AllocError>

    // optional hook (`::=`, no body). An arena no-ops this (frees
    // every page at once on drop); a malloc allocator does the real
    // thing. Neither is a stub.
    free* ::= <T>(self: @Self, p: Ptr<T>) ()

    // typed convenience: one T's worth of storage, pre-sized
    create* ::= <T>(self: @Self) Res<Ptr<T>, AllocError>

    // Vec, Map and String are NOT members — each is a ufcs function
    // beside its own type, taking an Alloc first (DESIGN.md), so
    // std.mem never sees inside a Vec and mem/collections don't
    // cross-import. Vec and Map return a BARE value, not a Res: empty
    // owns no pages, so construction can't fail. String is the honest
    // exception: it must hold a formatted result, so it allocates at
    // once.
}
